`,s.jsx(v,{items:[{q:"Expand l_t = alpha*y_t + (1-alpha)*l_{t-1} one step and say where the 'exponential' comes from.",a:"Substituting l_{t-1} gives alpha*y_t + alpha(1-alpha)*y_{t-1} + (1-alpha)^2 * l_{t-2}, and continuing shows the weight on a point k steps back is alpha*(1-alpha)^k — a geometric, i.e. exponentially decaying, sequence that never quite reaches zero."},{q:"Two questions pick your variant. What are they, and what does each answer buy you?",a:"Does the series trend? If yes you need Holt's b_t component, which turns a flat forecast into a sloped line. Does it have fixed-period seasonality? If yes you need Holt-Winters' s_t component, which adds a repeating per-period offset. No to both means SES suffices."},{q:"Someone runs SES on a strongly trending series. Describe the failure precisely.",a:"SES forecasts the last level, flat, forever. On an upward trend that is a systematically increasing under-forecast — the error grows linearly with the horizon rather than staying bounded. It is a bias, not noise, so no amount of data fixes it."},{q:"Why is fitting alpha, beta and gamma by minimising squared error the same as maximum likelihood?",a:"Under the assumption that the one-step errors are independent Gaussians with constant variance, the log-likelihood is a negative constant times the sum of squared errors. Maximising one is minimising the other — the same connection Module 8 of the maths track draws for MSE generally."}]}),`
